{"name":"Japanese Rose Garden","hex":"#e4b6c4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rose","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shrub of the genus Rosa, with red, pink, white or yellow flowers.","A flower of the rose plant."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make rose-colored; to redden or flush.","To perfume, as with roses."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rose"},{"term":"garden","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An outdoor area containing one or more types of plants, usually plants grown for food or ornamental purposes.","Such an ornamental place to which the public have access."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To grow plants in a garden; to create or maintain a garden.","Of a batsman, to inspect and tap the pitch lightly with the bat so as to smooth out small rough patches and irregularities."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/garden"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rose garden","blocks":[{"text":"A rose garden or rosarium is a garden or park, often open to the public, used to present and grow various types of garden roses, and sometimes rose species. Designs vary tremendously and roses may be displayed alongside other plants or grouped by individual variety, colour or class in rose beds. Technically it is a specialized type of shrub garden, but normally treated as a type of flower garden, if only because its origins in Europe go back to at least the Middle Ages in Europe, when roses were effectively the largest and most popular flowers, already existing in numerous garden cultivars."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Aramaki_rose_park04s2400.jpg/330px-Aramaki_rose_park04s2400.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose_garden","wikidata":"Q291177"},{"title":"Japanese rose","blocks":[{"text":"Japanese rose is a common name for several plants and may refer to:"},{"items":["Kerria japonica, native to China, Japan, and Korea","Rosa multiflora","Rosa rugosa"]}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_rose","wikidata":"Q6158999"},{"title":"Rose","blocks":[{"text":"A rose is either a woody perennial flowering plant of the genus Rosa, in the family Rosaceae, or the flower it bears. There are over three hundred species and tens of thousands of cultivars. The plants range in size and form, from trailing and erect shrubs to climbers up to 7 metres (23 ft) in height. Sharp prickles appear on the stems of many species. Their flowers vary in size and shape and are usually large and showy, in colours ranging from white through pinks, reds, oranges and yellows."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Rosa_rubiginosa_1.jpg/330px-Rosa_rubiginosa_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose","wikidata":"Q34687"},{"title":"Garden","blocks":[{"text":"A garden is a planned space, usually outdoors, set aside for the cultivation, display, and enjoyment of plants and other forms of nature. The single feature identifying even the wildest wild garden is control. The garden can incorporate both natural and artificial materials."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Brooklyn_Botanic_Garden_New_York_May_2015_010.jpg/330px-Brooklyn_Botanic_Garden_New_York_May_2015_010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garden","wikidata":"Q1107656"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Emilia_in_the_rosegarden_%28Teseida%29.jpg/330px-Emilia_in_the_rosegarden_%28Teseida%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Emilia_in_the_rosegarden_%28Teseida%29.jpg/330px-Emilia_in_the_rosegarden_%28Teseida%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Emilia in the rosegarden, Anjou, ~1460","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEmilia_in_the_rosegarden_(Teseida).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Jules_Gravereaux_toile.jpg/500px-Jules_Gravereaux_toile.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Jules_Gravereaux_toile.jpg/500px-Jules_Gravereaux_toile.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Jules Gravereaux in Roseraie du Val-de-Marne, 1900","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJules_Gravereaux_toile.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Ruston%27s_Rose_garden_2.JPG/500px-Ruston%27s_Rose_garden_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Ruston%27s_Rose_garden_2.JPG/500px-Ruston%27s_Rose_garden_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ruston's Roses in South Australia","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARuston's_Rose_garden_2.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Winterschutz.jpg/500px-Winterschutz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Winterschutz.jpg/1280px-Winterschutz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Winterschutz.jpg/500px-Winterschutz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Roses with protection against freezing – Volksgarten, Vienna","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWinterschutz.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Dickie-bandstand-3-3-2013.jpg/500px-Dickie-bandstand-3-3-2013